{
  "passage": "Most green plants make their own food, but a few plants also trap insects. The pitcher plant is one of them. It is found in the hills of Meghalaya and in some other parts of north-eastern India. Part of its leaf is shaped like a jug, or pitcher, and the tip of the leaf forms a lid over its mouth. The rim of the pitcher is slippery and often brightly coloured, and it produces a sweet substance that attracts insects. When an insect lands on the rim, it slips and falls inside. Hair inside the pitcher points downwards, so the insect cannot climb back out. At the bottom, the insect drowns in a liquid that contains digestive juices, and its body is slowly broken down and absorbed by the plant. Why should a green plant that can make its own food catch insects at all? The answer lies in the soil. Pitcher plants usually grow in soil that is poor in nitrogen, a nutrient that all plants need to make proteins. The insects supply the nitrogen that the soil lacks. So the pitcher plant still carries out photosynthesis in its green leaves; the insects are not its main source of food but a supplement. Other insect-eating plants include the Venus flytrap and the sundew.",
